# .trivyignore — Trivy vulnerability exemptions
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# How this file works
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Each line is a CVE ID (or OS package) that Trivy should suppress during
# filesystem scans.  Comments (lines starting with #) are ignored by Trivy
# but must be used to document WHY each exemption exists.
#
# This file is read automatically by the security-scan workflow via the
# `trivyignores` input.  Suppressed vulnerabilities will not fail the CI build
# and will not appear in the SARIF report uploaded to GitHub Code Scanning.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Exemption policy
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1.  Every entry MUST have a comment explaining the justification.
# 2.  Acceptable reasons to exempt:
#       a. False positive — the vulnerable code path is not used.
#       b.  No fix available — will be resolved upstream.
#       c.  Compensating control — risk mitigated by another measure.
#       d.  Accepted risk — documented business decision.
# 3.  Every entry should have an expiry/review date in the comment so stale
#     exemptions are revisited when dependencies are upgraded.
# 4.  Do NOT use this file to ignore vulnerabilities that should be fixed
#     by updating the dependency.  Update the dependency instead.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Format
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   <CVE-ID>  # <justification> | review-by: YYYY-MM-DD | added-by: <name>
#
# Example (uncomment if needed):
# CVE-2024-12345  # False positive: vulnerable function not called in our code | review-by: 2025-06-01 | added-by: devname
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
